    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Prove that\n$$\\cos 2x+\\cos 2y=2\\cos(x+y)\\cos(x-y)$$\nfor any $x$ and $y$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$2\\cos(x+y)\\cos(x-y)=\\cos 2x+\\cos 2y$.\n\nExpand the right-hand side as a difference of squares, then use the cosine double-angle formula.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}2\\cos(x+y)\\cos(x-y)&=2(\\cos x\\cos y-\\sin x\\sin y)(\\cos x\\cos y+\\sin x\\sin y)\\\\2\\cos(x+y)\\cos(x-y)&=2(\\cos^2x\\cos^2y-\\sin^2x\\sin^2y)\\\\2\\cos(x+y)\\cos(x-y)&=2\\bigl[\\cos^2x\\cos^2y-(1-\\cos^2x)(1-\\cos^2y)\\bigr]\\\\2\\cos(x+y)\\cos(x-y)&=2\\cos^2x+2\\cos^2y-2\\\\2\\cos(x+y)\\cos(x-y)&=(2\\cos^2x-1)+(2\\cos^2y-1)\\\\2\\cos(x+y)\\cos(x-y)&=\\cos 2x+\\cos 2y.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 52
  },
